{{also|amata}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From Latin ''[[amata]]'', ''loved'', ''beloved''; feminine perfect passive participle of ''[[amare]]'', ''love''. It can also be interpreted as the feminine form of the saint's name [[Amatus]].

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|s}}

# {{given name|female|from=Latin}}; always rare in English.
# {{context|Roman mythology|lang=en}} The wife of [[Latinus]] and the mother of [[Lavinia]].

